<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sun Yat-sen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sun Zhongshan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sun Yat-sen was a Chinese revolutionary and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> (Nationalist Party). After the 1911 revolution he became provisional president of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in January 1912 but soon gave way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a>. He later led rival governments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> warlords, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-principles-of-the-people">Three Principles of the People</a> made him the revered 'father of the nation' for both Nationalists and Communists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen.jpg" alt="Sun Yat-sen" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 上海波尔照相馆 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%AD%99%E4%B8%AD%E5%B1%B1%E8%82%96%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
